--TEST--
The four ways text refuses to behave like something it is not: a `string` subscripted, a `bytes` rendered without saying which encoding it is in, two strings ordered with `<`, and arithmetic over text (`rule:types/string-is-utf8`, `rule:types/bytes`, `rule:types/conversion`, `rule:types/arithmetic`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:types/text-string-and-bytes

// `rule:types/string-is-utf8`: a `string` is measured in extended grapheme
// clusters, and a subscript names a position in something whose elements are
// all the same width, which text is not. `Core\Str::at` and `Core\Str::slice`
// are the spelling, and they answer whole characters.
string $s = "grüße";
echo $s[0], "\n";

// `rule:types/bytes`: octets carry no encoding, so there is no text they
// obviously are. `echo` converts what it is given to `string`, and
// `rule:types/conversion` makes that direction the checked one, so it has to
// be written out rather than assumed.
bytes $b = "nvs" as bytes;
echo $b, "\n";

// `rule:types/arithmetic` tabulates no ordering for text: PHP's `<` over two
// strings converts them first, and `Core\Str::compare` is the ordering that
// says out loud what it compares.
string $a = "a";
string $z = "b";
echo $a < $z, "\n";

// And no arithmetic row either, where PHP reads a leading number out of the
// text and drops the rest.
string $n = "3";
echo $n * 2, "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0482]: `string` cannot be subscripted
%A
error[E0707]: `bytes` has no string form
%A
error[E0715]: `<` has no meaning for `string`
%A
error[E0716]: `*` has no meaning for `string`
%A
error: aborting due to 4 errors
